?","options":["A straight line with slope $-1$ through the origin.","A single point only.","A circle centred at the origin.","The empty set."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"4aad233ae28bc1ae07f35a6f","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_level_set_and_level_curve_929acfce_008","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"For $f(x,y) = x+y$, which statement describes its level curves?","options":["They are circles centred at the origin.","They are parallel straight lines of slope $-1$.","They are single points for every $k$.","They are empty for every $k$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c76aad838ae8ca2746df1c04","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_level_set_and_level_curve_929acfce_009","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why can one point not lie in both $L_1(f)$ and $L_2(f)$?","options":["That would require $f(\\boldsymbol{x}) = 1$ and $f(\\boldsymbol{x}) = 2$ at the same point.","Because level sets are defined only for positive values.","Because level sets are never subsets of the domain.","Because singleton sets always contain two values."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"7a70d174d701e42be88112d8","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_level_set_and_level_curve_929acfce_010","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which prerequisite idea is directly used in $L_k(f)=f^{-1}(\\{k\\})$?","options":["Matrix multiplication.","Preimage of a singleton set.","Uniform convergence.","Metric balls."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"8d033cfca2af46096673ee84","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"no_diagnostic_value"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_level_set_and_level_curve_929acfce_011","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"For $f(x,y)=x^2+y^2$, why is $L_{-1}(f)$ empty over $\\mathbb{R}^2$ but not a useful template over every possible domain?","options":["Level sets are never allowed to be empty.","The value $-1$ must first be rounded to $0$.","The notation $L_k(f)$ only applies to linear functions.","The conclusion uses that real squares are non-negative."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"e5e5ed5d96bc08ab6e607315","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_level_set_and_level_curve_6b3a1cc4_013","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"How is the level set $L_k(f)$ defined?","options":["$L_k(f)=\\{k\\colon f(k)=0\\}$","$L_k(f)=\\mathbb{R}^n\\setminus X$","$L_k(f)=\\{\\boldsymbol{x}\\colon f(\\boldsymbol{x})=k\\}$","$L_k(f)=\\{\\boldsymbol{x}\\colon f(\\boldsymbol{x})\\neq k\\}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"5a06fe2515901f5fb0664db0","time_limit":20,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_level_set_and_level_curve_6b3a1cc4_014","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For functions of two variables, what is a level set called?","options":["A level surface, because its points lie in the plane $\\mathbb{R}^2$.","The graph of the function, a surface in $\\mathbb{R}^3$.","A level curve, also known as a contour line.","A hyperplane of $\\mathbb{R}^2$, that is, a straight line."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"d5f4331a3c14f65ad9b1e4ef","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-